e acquired  a lot of words and it's been exposed to  a lot of the language through as you  heard me say before input activities so  if I look at my Arabic learning the sort  of first invitation to learn Arabic was  when I was at the polyglot conference in  Bratislava I met a gentleman who worked  with icy mill in Germany and I was  chatting with him and I his name is  Philippe I think yeah he'll correct me  if I'm wrong I said I looked it up  beforehand and he said this is the best  book and and I had said one of the  obstacles to me was the writing system  and he said well this is a great way to  learn it so I bought the US email system  from him he gave me a bit of a break I  looked into this book and I just found  it overwhelming I couldn't read the  Arabic writing they introduced the  letters one by one there was a lot that  it not to mention the fact that it's  written for German speakers and so  therefore the kind of collet  romanization the pronunciation is for  German speakers so they have no way of  showing the the t the the and thought  sounds in Arabic so they used some funny  combination of German that was further  confusing then on top of that to  introduce some niceties of the grammar I  left this book just left it at home  didn't even bother with it but I'll come  back to it and that's what I'm going to  talk about later on here so then I had a  quick overview of No then my friend  Judith Mayer who's a great polyglot she  suggested I try her book which I went  through learn to read Arabic quickly  master a new alphabet well you don't  quickly master any of the basics in a  language you start to get introduced to  these things I didn't with all due  respect to Judith and I think it depends  on how you learn and many people love  this book I just couldn't do it because  the letters were taught with reference  to names of countries cognates think  that we're familiar with so if we see  the name  Cambodia and then we see that written in  Arabic and that's gonna help us learn  the letters I didn't find that I just  couldn't do that I didn't want to look  at a bunch of names of countries and  cities and stuff like that so this  didn't help me what I found eventually  was that I just had to get in the  language and so as those of you who have  been following you know that I  downloaded some tables of the letters of  the Arabic alphabet and slowly  painstakingly started reading I found  out that I could actually type quite  easily on my iPad in Arabic so that  after a while I started getting closer  and closer to you know a sense of what  these letters were because they appear  in different forms I mean it was  difficult and it continues to be  difficult and but at least now I can  kind of read and of course I listen and  so I have read I can remember the  statistic twenty or thirty thousand  words in Arabic I have listened to a  hundred hours of Arabic 9100 I can  remember I have listened to these sixty  many stories that's been my main  activity just about the only thing I do  I listen to them thirty forty talent  times some of them lessons one to twenty  I know quite well twenty to thirty quite  well thirty to forty bit of a struggle  forty to fifty a little more difficult  than 50 and Beyond is even more  difficult but I know because I have  overcome the difficulty of the first ten  and the first twenty and then the first  thirty that I will eventually get to the  sixty and there is a lot of vocabulary  there and Allah essentially all the  basic structures of the language are  there and I have heard them but in  hearing them and in reading there's a  lot of stuff that I don't notice so and  a lot of stuff that I wonder about why  does it you know go this way I know what  the story means in fact and this is a  suggestion one thing that's very useful  to do after you have read this I read  the story in Arabic and I've saved words  and phrases to my database at  used some words and phrases there's  still a few issues relating to tents etc  that a little bit fuzzy  then I listen to the story typically if  I'm on my stepper at home and I read the  translation because all of these stories  have English and not only Incas are  translated into many languages so I can  listen to the story which I listened to  before many times which I've read in  Arabic and now I can listen to it and  read it in English and that helps  clarify certain things about that story  so that when I next listen to it I have  a better sense of what's happening not  only the meaning but also the structure  of the language then I then went back to  my a Samia book and struggled with their  funny romanization struggle with some  other things but what they do in this  book is the introduced the basics so now  they go through explaining some of the  endings of words some of the tenses some  of the you know prepositions what we  would call prepositions which often are  slammed together with the word in Arabic  and how all of that works and so as I do  that then I get a better sense now I can  cope with the basics because I have a  point of reference I've been listening  to and reading all this stuff so I can  have a bit of a sense just an almost  intuitive sense of how the language  works I have a lot of words I can now  read that was another thing when you  start into for example even this book  which I glossed over the first time all  the examples they give you are in Arabic  so if I'm not that comfortable reading  in Arabic that's a block right away I  should add by the way that some of the  books I bought on Arabic the Arabic  script is so small that it's ridiculous  as a learner you want larger script  another advantage of learning on an iPad  is you can adjust the font size at any  rate  now that I can read Arabic when they  give some example verbs I can actually  read it comfortably plus I've seen the  verb and different verb forms in  different texts that I've been listening  to and reading so when I read an  